# Logs
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
lerna-debug.log*

node_modules
dist
dist-ssr
.output
.vinxi
.tanstack/**
.nitro
*.local

# Runtime configuration belongs in deployment/local environment stores. Keep a
# documentation-only example in git; never commit environment-specific values.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Wrangler / Cloudflare
.wrangler/
.dev.vars

# Editor directories and files
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea
.DS_Store
*.suo
*.ntvs*
*.njsproj
*.sln
*.sw?

# Generated managed-browser sidecars. CI/release builds recreate the
# target-specific executable and Tauri bundles it through externalBin.
.browser-sidecar/
native/companion/tauri/binaries/ocade-browser-host-*
.gesture-sidecar/
native/companion/tauri/binaries/ocade-gesture-host-*
native/companion/tauri/target/
native/companion/tauri/gen/
# The safety crate builds to its own target directory, which this list did not
# cover — so 34 MB of Rust build output sat in `git status` as untracked files
# on every run, buried under the ones a person actually needed to read.
native/companion/safety/target/

# Pytest and Ruff caches. Regenerated by the next run of either tool.
native/python-host/.pytest_cache/
native/python-host/.ruff_cache/

# Python bytecode, ANYWHERE.
#
# The previous rule was scoped to `native/python-host/**`, which left the
# incoming control engine uncovered: the first time anyone runs it, it writes
# `__pycache__` directories that would show up as new files to commit inside a
# directory that is supposed to be held exactly as delivered. Generated
# bytecode is never source, wherever it lands.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# TanStack Router's local build cache.
.tanstack/

# PRIVATE KEY MATERIAL. NEVER COMMITTED, ANYWHERE, FOR ANY REASON.
#
# The incoming control engine ships `config/certs/jarvis.key` — a real private
# key — and the untracked-path classifier caught it on its first run. That
# directory is read-only in this pass, so the key is not being moved or
# deleted; this makes it impossible to commit by accident instead, which is the
# part that would actually be irreversible. Broadened past that one file
# because the next key to arrive will not be called jarvis.key.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
**/certs/

# The OCADE-owned Python environment. The PINNED REQUIREMENTS are tracked
# (native/python-host/requirements.txt); the installed tree is not — it is
# ~400 MB of platform-specific wheels that setup recreates on each machine.
native/python-host/.venv/
native/python-host/.venv-gesture/
native/python-host/models/
native/python-host/**/__pycache__/

# Licence signing key material — never commit
ocade-licence.*
windows-proof-evidence.json
# ONE PATTERN, NOT A LIST THAT HAS TO BE REMEMBERED.
#
# These were enumerated individually, so every new end-to-end harness that
# picked its own build directory was untracked only until somebody added it
# here -- and until then `git add -A` committed a whole production build. That
# is exactly what happened: .output-diag and .output-firstrun, 1,148 build
# artifacts, went in on a commit that was about something else entirely.
#
# A harness choosing a new output directory is normal and should not require a
# corresponding edit here.
#
# (And these are `#` comments, because .gitignore has no block comments. The
# first attempt used /* ... */, which git would have read as patterns -- with
# `/*` meaning "ignore everything in the repository root".)
.output-*
.proof
public/models/*.gguf

release/launch-proof-logs/
.gate-logs

# Disposable inspection/extraction space. Donor and package probes are never
# production inputs and must remain outside the tracked/release tree.
.work/

# The browser-engine harness compiles product code into a bundle and removes
# it again; a crashed run must not leave one to be committed.
scripts/.engine-probe.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------- donor archive --
# PLAN.md §25: "Remove the raw RAR from the final tracked product and releases
# without rewriting history."
#
# The archive was dispositioned in full — every one of its 2,126 files has a
# decided outcome in `imports/ocade-primary-logic/INTEGRATION_MAP.csv`, and the
# licence and provenance records it required are kept. What is removed is the
# ten-megabyte binary itself, which the product does not read, no test opens,
# and the report generator never touches: it works from the CSV ledger.
#
# IGNORED RATHER THAN ONLY DELETED, because `.incoming/` is a live drop point.
# Deleting the file without this rule means the next archive placed there is
# committed by the next `git add -A`, and the requirement quietly comes undone.
# History is untouched: the archive and its hash remain in earlier commits,
# which is where provenance belongs.
.incoming/*.rar
.route-qa-evidence/
supabase/.temp/
